// The task adapter world: one media task's lifecycle, as pure translation.
//
// A chat call is one request and one response. A media task is a lifecycle —
// submit, observe until terminal, render — with a durable row, a lease, a
// reservation and a settlement between the steps. Those belong to the host.
// What lives here is the dialect half: how this provider's wire expresses each
// stage, and nothing about when to run one.
//
// The discipline is the provider world's, unchanged: **the component
// describes, the host executes.** Every function is pure. A component has no
// network, no clock and no memory across calls, so it can neither send its own
// request nor decide that time has run out.
//
// Two absences are deliberate and recorded (2026-09-18 task-adapter-world
// record, D2):
//
//   - no `timed-out`: a waiting budget is host policy, and neither side may
//     synthesise a terminal from a clock (2026-08-27 vocabulary record, D3
//     rule 5). A local deadline yields `unknown`, which is a reconciliation
//     input and not an outcome.
//   - no `usage-intent`: metering is the component's and pricing the host's,
//     but a metering vocabulary is admitted only with an executing consumer.
//     It lands with the host seam, as a later package minor.

package token-station:task-adapter@1.0.0;

/// Identity and liveness. Structurally identical to the provider world's, and
/// deliberately not shared: the two packages version independently, and a
/// shared type would couple them again through the back door.
interface common {
    record adapter-metadata {
        name: string,
        version: string,
        /// Must equal the world this component was built against:
        /// `task-adapter-v1`.
        api-version: string,
    }

    enum health-status {
        ready,
        degraded,
        unavailable,
    }

    record adapter-health {
        status: health-status,
        detail: option<string>,
    }
}

/// Credential operations the host performs on a task component's behalf.
///
/// Identical in shape and in rule to the provider world's `host`: the
/// component passes the *name* of a credential and the host resolves it;
/// everything returned here is not a credential.
///
/// The task half resolves that name against a **pre-resolved** secret
/// (`PreparedSecretResolverV1`), never a routing resolver — polling and
/// artifact fetch must run under the credential the task was *submitted*
/// with, and a chat-shaped resolver's weight-and-failover default is exactly
/// the reported failure mode (2026-08-27 vocabulary record, D1). That is a
/// host obligation; the component cannot express it and is not asked to.
interface host {
    /// `secret-ref` is a name declared in the component's manifest under
    /// `permissions.secrets`. Signing a name the manifest did not declare
    /// fails.
    ///
    /// The signature is not a credential, so the component may place it in an
    /// ordinary header or the body. It may *not* place it in `authorization`
    /// or any other credential header: the descriptor's safe-header rule
    /// refuses those names.
    sign: func(secret-ref: string, payload: list<u8>, algorithm: string) -> result<list<u8>, string>;
}

/// One provider family's task dialect: three stages, seven functions, all pure.
interface task-adapter {
    use common.{adapter-metadata, adapter-health};

    /// A JSON document. The doc comment on each use names its canonical type.
    type json = string;

    metadata: func() -> adapter-metadata;
    healthcheck: func() -> adapter-health;

    // ── Stage one: submit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// `provider-config`: `protocol::ProviderConfig` (the policy-fenced
    /// four-field subset) · `task-request`: the caller's normalised request ·
    /// `host-minted`: `south_contracts::HostMintedValuesV1`
    /// -> `protocol::HttpRequestDescriptor`.
    ///
    /// `host-minted` carries the values only the host can produce and only the
    /// dialect knows where to put: the gateway task id (Kling embeds it as
    /// `external_task_id`, which is what makes a resubmission idempotent
    /// upstream) and, where the provider supports one, a callback URL already
    /// bearing the host's nonce. Three rules ride with the type
    /// (2026-08-27 vocabulary record, D2):
    ///
    ///   1. **The component places them; it never invents them.** A component
    ///      that generates its own task id destroys the host's idempotency
    ///      anchor — the upstream accepts the resubmission as new work and the
    ///      reservation pays for both.
    ///   2. **They are opaque strings.** Do not parse the callback URL, derive
    ///      anything from the id, or reorder their bytes. A dialect with no
    ///      callback concept ignores the option.
    ///   3. **The nonce plaintext exists only inside that URL.** Never echo it
    ///      into an error message: the error channel is an `ErrorEnvelope`
    ///      precisely so free text cannot leak it.
    build-submit-request: func(
        provider-config: json,
        task-request: json,
        host-minted: json,
    ) -> result<json, json>;

    /// How a submit attempt ended, as the dialect reads it off the wire.
    ///
    /// A closed set, and deliberately **not** `result<string, _>`: two of these
    /// four have no representation in "an id or an error", and one of the two
    /// is funds-critical (2026-09-19 fit survey, D1).
    variant submit-outcome {
        /// The upstream accepted and issued a task id. The host stores it on
        /// the durable row and hands it back to `build-observe-request`.
        accepted(string),
        /// Submit *was* the terminal answer. Some dialects answer a creation
        /// synchronously and issue no pollable id at all — the payload is the
        /// terminal body, which the host feeds straight to its terminal entry.
        accepted-terminal(json),
        /// HTTP 2xx carrying a business-layer refusal. The payload is a
        /// `protocol::ErrorEnvelope`: the upstream took the call and declined
        /// the work, so nothing is running and the host may release.
        rejected(json),
        /// The response shape is unrecognised. **The upstream may have
        /// accepted the work and may be billing for it**, so the host keeps
        /// the reservation and lets reconciliation settle it.
        ///
        /// This variant is why the outcome is not a `result`. "We cannot tell"
        /// is not "it failed": folding it into the error arm tells the host to
        /// release a reservation against work that may be running — the submit
        /// side of the mistake D3 rule 5 forbids on the observe side.
        unknown,
    }

    /// `response-parts`: `protocol::HttpResponseParts` -> [`submit-outcome`].
    ///
    /// The outer `result` means the component could not process the response
    /// at all, which is distinct from every variant above.
    parse-submit-response: func(response-parts: json) -> result<submit-outcome, json>;

    // ── Stage two: observe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// `provider-config`: `protocol::ProviderConfig` · `upstream-model`: the
    /// model this task was submitted with · `upstream-task-id`: what
    /// `parse-submit-response` returned -> `protocol::HttpRequestDescriptor`.
    ///
    /// A GET, buffered (`GetRequestV1`, HTTP contract six). The host owns the
    /// polling cadence, the lease and the backoff; this function owns only
    /// where to look.
    ///
    /// **Why the model is a parameter** (2026-09-19 fit survey, D3): one
    /// provider can serve text-to-video, image-to-video and motion control on
    /// three different paths, and which one a task used is per-task state, not
    /// provider configuration. A component given only the task id polls the
    /// wrong endpoint for two of those three. `provider-config.models` cannot
    /// answer it — it lists what the provider serves, not what this task sent.
    build-observe-request: func(
        provider-config: json,
        upstream-model: string,
        upstream-task-id: string,
    ) -> result<json, json>;

    /// `response-parts`: `protocol::HttpResponseParts`
    /// -> `south_contracts::TaskObservationV1`
    /// (`running | succeeded | failed(kind) | unknown`).
    ///
    /// The rules are frozen (2026-08-27 vocabulary record, D3):
    ///
    ///   - **`provider-expired` requires an explicit upstream statement.**
    ///     Measured across six families, only BytePlus and xAI say it on the
    ///     wire; everything else that resembles expiry is a plain failure or
    ///     `unknown`. There is no `expired` observation variant — expiry is a
    ///     failure *kind*.
    ///   - **An unrecognised status word is `unknown`**, never a synthesised
    ///     failure. Inventing a terminal renders "we cannot tell" as "it is
    ///     over".
    ///   - **A non-2xx query is `unknown`.** A failed *query* is not a failed
    ///     *task*: 429, 5xx, 404 and 401 all mean the observation did not
    ///     happen. A family whose upstream expresses expiry only as a 404 stays
    ///     `unknown` — a 404 is also what a wrong credential returns — and the
    ///     host's reconciliation settles it.
    ///   - **Retry policy is not expressed here.** The host reads it off the
    ///     kind; there is no `retriable` flag.
    parse-observation: func(response-parts: json) -> result<json, json>;

    // ── Stage three: render ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// `provider-config`: `protocol::ProviderConfig` · `observation`: the
    /// terminal `south_contracts::TaskObservationV1`
    /// -> `option<protocol::HttpRequestDescriptor>`.
    ///
    /// **Why `provider-config`** (2026-09-19 fit survey, D2): the one family
    /// this function exists for builds its retrieve URL from the provider's
    /// host and API version, and decides whether a fetch is needed at all by
    /// looking at the model generation. Without the config it cannot build the
    /// only request it is here to build.
    ///
    /// **`none` means "the observation already has everything", not "not
    /// supported".** Five of six measured families are `none`; MiniMax is the
    /// exception, whose terminal observation carries only a `file_id` whose
    /// download URL takes another call.
    ///
    /// This is the pair that keeps rendering pure. The component *declares*
    /// where to look, the host performs the fetch, and the bytes come back
    /// through `render-success`. A component that fetched its own artifact
    /// would need a network, which it does not have.
    ///
    /// A component that can return `some` declares `artifact_fetch` in its
    /// manifest, so a host knows before the first call whether to wire that
    /// path.
    build-artifact-request: func(
        provider-config: json,
        observation: json,
    ) -> result<json, json>;

    /// `observation`: the terminal `south_contracts::TaskObservationV1` ·
    /// `fetched`: the `protocol::HttpResponseParts` of the artifact request,
    /// or `none` when the previous function asked for nothing ·
    /// `host-minted`: `south_contracts::HostMintedValuesV1`
    /// -> the success body.
    ///
    /// Pure by construction: every input arrives as a parameter.
    ///
    /// **Why `host-minted` reaches the render side too** (2026-09-19 fit
    /// survey, D4): artifacts are addressed by the host's own relative path,
    /// built from the gateway task id, and the component must place that id in
    /// the body it renders. It is the same "place, never invent" rule the
    /// submit side already carries — and the same type, so no new vocabulary.
    ///
    /// That id is also what replaced the credential a renderer used to need:
    /// a component that had to build an absolute artifact URL would need the
    /// upstream's key, which it can never see.
    render-success: func(
        observation: json,
        fetched: option<json>,
        host-minted: json,
    ) -> result<json, json>;

    /// `observation`: a terminal failure `south_contracts::TaskObservationV1`
    /// -> `protocol::ErrorEnvelope`.
    ///
    /// Must map onto the closed `protocol::ErrorCode` catalog: the host
    /// branches on it to decide whether another upstream may be tried. An
    /// unmappable failure is `internal`, never an invented code. Whether to
    /// try another upstream is the host's decision and is never expressed
    /// here.
    map-terminal-failure: func(observation: json) -> result<json, json>;
}

/// World name doubles as the manifest's `api_version`.
///
/// Imports `host` so it can name credentials. Exports no way to read one.
world task-adapter-v1 {
    import host;
    export task-adapter;
}
